 ## Overview
-Out-of-core point-based rendering is a method for the interactive visualization of very large volumetric datasets that bypasses the memory limitations of traditional in-core techniques by storing voxel data on disk [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time interaction with high-resolution data by assuming voxel density is sufficient to represent isovalue structures as discrete points, eliminating the need for connectivity or topology information [S1, unofficial]. Unlike mesh-based methods which fail to provide interactive frame rates at full resolution for large datasets, this approach renders contours and volume approximations directly from sorted point clouds [S1, unofficial].
+Out-of-core point-based rendering is a method for the interactive visualization of very large volumetric datasets that exceed available main memory [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time interaction with high-resolution data by assuming that voxel density is sufficient to represent isovalue structures (contours) using discrete points rather than continuous meshes or full volume textures [S1, unofficial]. Unlike mesh-based methods which require connectivity and topology information, this approach renders points directly, allowing for full-resolution contour rendering at interactive frame rates without the computational overhead of surface extraction [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Specific Data Formats and Primitives](7148-specific-data-formats-and-primitives.md) by utilizing out-of-core storage and point-based primitives.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A gridded volumetric dataset (scalar field or RGB color volume) of arbitrary size, limited only by disk space and OS file size constraints.
-*   **Output:** A rendered image of point primitives representing specific isovalue sets, updated interactively.
+**Inputs:** A gridded volumetric dataset (scalar field) and a target color space (typically RGB).
+**Outputs:** A single file containing sorted voxel data and a look-up table, enabling interactive retrieval and rendering of specific isovalue sets.
 
